From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:24:04 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] speakers: end every output path with a true-peak
 brick-wall limiter

The compressor that ends the shared speaker graph shape (woofer_lim:
0 dBFS, 15:1, 1 ms attack) lets transients through. The amps take
integer samples, so whatever leaves the graph at or over full scale is
hard-clipped at the conversion, before the amp's own gain, where
speakerguardd and speakersafetyd cannot act on it. On the J616s at
100 % the stock graph's woofer feed went over full scale 410-450 times
in 30 s of bass-heavy music, peaking at +5 dBFS, and even -12 dBFS pink
noise clipped. That is the crackle or pop on bass hits.

Rendered offline (the graphs in a private PipeWire daemon, no ALSA),
every graph but the J700's clips the same way, the woofers on all of
them and the tweeters on j314 and j415 too:

  graph   30 s of music, clip events   peak    -12 dBFS pink
  j274    23 / 8                      +2.0    0
  j293    93 / 86 / 136 / 130         +5.5    0
  j313    556 / 586                   +7.0    370
  j314    240 / 244, tweeters 8 / 14  +2.5    0
  j316    412 / 452, tweeters 0 / 3   +5.1    1
  j375    452 / 452                   +5.2    52
  j413    245 / 267                   +7.0    142
  j415    184 / 181, tweeters 75 / 70 +4.5    16
  j616    412 / 452                   +5.1    1
  j700    0                           -0.5    0

End each output path with an LSP limiter (threshold -1 dBFS, true-peak
oversampling, 5 ms lookahead, 1 ms attack, 20 ms release, no level
regulation or boost). Every path of a graph gets the same limiter, so
woofers and tweeters keep their relative alignment: the limiter adds
364 frames (7.6 ms) to each path. With it no graph clips on any test
signal and the peaks are at -1 dBFS. On the music the woofer level is
0.3-0.7 dB lower than what the stock graphs delivered after the
clipping (0.2-0.35 dB A-weighted), the tweeters are unchanged, and a
sustained 30-300 Hz sweep at -3 dBFS, which the stock graphs squared
off, loses 0.5-1 dB. Two limiters cost the J616s about 4 % of a core.

Played on the J616s at 100 % (speakerguardd governing, the graph's
output tapped digitally, the internal microphone recording): the woofer
feed went from 409/449 clip events and +5.05 dBFS peaks to none at
-1.00 dBFS on the same 30 s clip, and the microphone level changed by
-0.1 dB(A), inside the 0.5 dB run-to-run spread of two stock runs
bracketing it.

The J700 graph already ends with a limiter and does not clip; it is
unchanged.

